#!/bin/sh
# Concurrency guard. See decisions/0021-committing-under-concurrency.md.
#
# More than one agent session writes these repositories at the same time, and the
# damage from `git add -A` is not lost work, it is a commit message that describes
# half of what the commit contains. Commit cdc0e52 in the private repository is the
# worked example.
#
# This hook cannot tell an intended multi-area commit from a sweep: only the person
# committing knows which paths were theirs. So it does not guess. It enforces that
# the decision was made deliberately, by requiring commits to come through
# `kb commit`, which takes an explicit pathspec and reads the commit back.
#
# The escape hatch is one environment variable and it is not hidden, because a guard
# with no way past it gets disabled entirely and then guards nothing.

if [ -n "$KB_COMMIT" ]; then
    exit 0
fi

if [ -n "$KB_ALLOW_RAW_COMMIT" ]; then
    echo "kb: raw git commit allowed by KB_ALLOW_RAW_COMMIT." >&2
    echo "kb: staging exactly these paths:" >&2
    git diff --cached --name-only | sed 's/^/kb:   /' >&2
    exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<'MSG'
kb: refusing a raw `git commit` in a repository more than one session writes.

  Use:      kb commit <path>... -m "message"
  Or:       KB_ALLOW_RAW_COMMIT=1 git commit ...

`kb commit` takes an explicit pathspec, so another session's in-flight work cannot
be swept into your commit, and it reads the commit back afterwards to prove that
nothing else moved. There is deliberately no flag meaning everything.

Why this is not advice: `git commit -- <paths>` builds the commit from only those
paths and ignores the rest of the index. A plain `git commit` takes whatever is
staged, including what another session staged one second ago.
MSG
exit 1
